Lab Environment Overview
Health probes in OpenShift Virtualization work similarly to Kubernetes pod probes but are specifically designed for virtual machines. They help ensure VMs are running correctly and can automatically restart unhealthy VMs.

Key Concepts:

Readiness Probe: Determines if a VM is ready to receive traffic
Liveness Probe: Determines if a VM is running properly and should be restarted if unhealthy
Probe Types: TCP, HTTP, and guest agent probes
Task 1: Set Up Readiness and Liveness Probes for VMs
Subtask 1.1: Create a Base Virtual Machine
First, let's create a virtual machine that we'll configure with health probes.

Access your OpenShift environment
# Verify you're logged into OpenShift
oc whoami

# Create a new project for this lab
oc new-project vm-health-probes

# Verify the project was created
oc project vm-health-probes
Create a virtual machine with a web server
Create a file called vm-with-webserver.yaml:

apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: webserver-vm
  namespace: vm-health-probes
  labels:
    app: webserver
spec:
  running: true
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: webserver-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        devices:
          disks:
          - name: containerdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
          - name: cloudinitdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
          interfaces:
          - name: default
            masquerade: {}
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
            cpu: 1
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - name: containerdisk
        containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/fedora-with-test-tooling-container-disk:latest
      - name: cloudinitdisk
        cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
            packages:
              - nginx
            runcmd:
              - systemctl enable nginx
              - systemctl start nginx
              - echo "<h1>Health Probe Test Server</h1><p>VM is healthy!</p>" > /var/www/html/index.html
              - echo "<h1>Health Check</h1><p>Status: OK</p>" > /var/www/html/health
Apply the VM configuration
# Create the virtual machine
oc apply -f vm-with-webserver.yaml

# Wait for the VM to start
oc get vm webserver-vm -w

# Check the VM instance status
oc get vmi webserver-vm
Subtask 1.2: Configure HTTP Readiness Probe
Now let's add a readiness probe that checks if the web server is responding.

Create VM with HTTP readiness probe
Create a file called vm-with-readiness-probe.yaml:

apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: webserver-vm-probes
  namespace: vm-health-probes
  labels:
    app: webserver-probes
spec:
  running: true
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: webserver-vm-probes
    spec:
      domain:
        devices:
          disks:
          - name: containerdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
          - name: cloudinitdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
          interfaces:
          - name: default
            masquerade: {}
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
            cpu: 1
      readinessProbe:
        httpGet:
          path: /health
          port: 80
          scheme: HTTP
        initialDelaySeconds: 60
        periodSeconds: 10
        timeoutSeconds: 5
        successThreshold: 1
        failureThreshold: 3
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - name: containerdisk
        containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/fedora-with-test-tooling-container-disk:latest
      - name: cloudinitdisk
        cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
            packages:
              - nginx
            runcmd:
              - systemctl enable nginx
              - systemctl start nginx
              - echo "<h1>Health Probe Test Server</h1><p>VM is healthy!</p>" > /var/www/html/index.html
              - echo "<h1>Health Check</h1><p>Status: OK</p>" > /var/www/html/health
Apply the configuration
# Delete the previous VM if it exists
oc delete vm webserver-vm --ignore-not-found=true

# Create the new VM with readiness probe
oc apply -f vm-with-readiness-probe.yaml

# Monitor the VM startup
oc get vmi webserver-vm-probes -w
