Task 3: Monitor VM Health Using OpenShift Console and CLI
Subtask 3.1: Monitor Health via CLI
Let's explore various CLI commands to monitor VM health status.

Use oc commands to check VM health
# Get comprehensive VM status
oc get vmi --show-labels

# Get detailed VM information including probe status
oc describe vmi webserver-vm-full-probes

# Check VM conditions
oc get vmi webserver-vm-full-probes -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions}' | jq '.'

# Monitor VM events in real-time
oc get events -w --field-selector involvedObject.name=webserver-vm-full-probes
Create a monitoring script
Create a file called monitor-vm-health.sh:

#!/bin/bash

VM_NAME="webserver-vm-full-probes"
NAMESPACE="vm-health-probes"

echo "=== VM Health Monitor ==="
echo "Monitoring VM: $VM_NAME in namespace: $NAMESPACE"
echo "Press Ctrl+C to stop"
echo

while true; do
    clear
    echo "=== VM Status at $(date) ==="
    
    # Get VM basic status
    echo "VM Status:"
    oc get vmi $VM_NAME -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,PHASE:.status.phase,READY:.status.conditions[?(@.type==\"Ready\")].status,AGE:.metadata.creationTimestamp
    
    echo
    echo "Probe Status:"
    # Check readiness probe
    READY_STATUS=$(oc get vmi $VM_NAME -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions[?(@.type=="Ready")].status}')
    echo "Readiness Probe: $READY_STATUS"
    
    # Get recent events
    echo
    echo "Recent Events:"
    oc get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp' --field-selector involvedObject.name=$VM_NAME | tail -5
    
    sleep 10
done
Make the script executable and run it
# Make the script executable
chmod +x monitor-vm-health.sh

# Run the monitoring script
./monitor-vm-health.sh
